#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447202 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447202 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "74ad51d2-25d4-59e0-a633-9b2e1070afdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447203 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b9ef3f22-a619-5b50-8f97-3dc74dbda9d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447204 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e27dc486-028e-5b85-8121-cb4f3fc8a15b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1071 "A processor manufacturer determines that $2%$ of the processors it produces are defective. A quality control officer inspects a random sample of $500$ processors. What is the standard deviation of the number of defective processors found in the sample? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bcd51a1-0665-5138-a53c-48e05f564d89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447205 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective processors.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=500$ processors\n\n- the probability of a processor being defective is $p=2%=0.02$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(500,0.02).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of defective processors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(500)(0.02)(1-0.02)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9.8} \\\\  & =3.13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair $8$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $8$ is rolled $12$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47805108-6203-5bb3-98d1-dea670ffc279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447206 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ or $8$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =1.732\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair spinner is designed so that $30%$ of its surface area is painted in red. Tom spins the spinner $15$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times the spinner lands on red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b0b4129-44a0-54cb-bf0f-c1b1c637d428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447207 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red is $p=30%=0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,0.3).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times the spinner lands on red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(15)(0.3)(1-0.3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3.15} \\\\  & =1.775\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that $40%$ of a given population have blood type O$+.$ What is the variance of the number of people in a random sample of $100$ people having blood type O$+?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66116800-3925-5364-86bb-866dbabfb7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447208 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people having blood type O$+.$\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=100$ people\n\n- the probability of finding a person with blood type O$+$ is $p=40%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(100,0.4).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of people with blood type O$+$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(100)(0.4)(1-0.4) \\\\  & =(40)(0.6) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1071 "A new drug has a $75%$ probability of curing a patient within a week. If $50$ patients are treated, what is the variance of the number of cured patients within a week?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca0fbb1f-e0c9-5b4f-9fe5-ceb4b1f414a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447209 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug cures a patient within a week is $p=75%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.75).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(1-0.75) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(0.25) \\\\  & =9.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447210 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447210 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "048a1307-200e-5108-a025-827c957dc173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447210 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447211 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447211 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "281fc6c5-f0d6-53e7-acdb-64ce6dd2027e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447211 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447212 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447212 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a403e6a-cb61-53cc-8787-e8ddb2f89be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447212 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447213 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e456f34-784c-5b15-be45-6dc17016e9ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447213 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447214 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447214 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b808605-f501-5056-8071-6d7f1c92fb43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447214 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447207 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
